The credentials initially allowed their holder to list and read objects from any S3 bucket in Copperline’s account. That included freight manifests with consignee details, dispatch logs, CloudTrail records and migration scratch files. Someone could expose customer and shipment information, study operational patterns, or use the same identity’s policy-version permissions to restore broad access after it was narrowed. We removed the wildcard grant, kept access to ops logs, and verified that scratch listing is now denied. We did not read the scratch-file contents.

We did not inspect everything. Calls using these credentials to enumerate EC2 instances and inspect organization policies were denied. That means we cannot say whether instances or organization-wide restrictions exist. A wider review needs an approved read-only audit role that can enumerate those areas and inspect the organization’s applied policies.

There is a separate provider boundary. Copperline controls its users, policies, data, and settings for storage, queues, keys and secrets—even when AWS operates the service. AWS operates the datacenters, hardware and underlying managed-service infrastructure; those were outside this account review and were not tested. Use AWS assurance materials and contractual commitments to assess those provider controls.
